# tools/r/rule_twin_witness.rish -- a rule and its editor twin say the same thing.
#
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/r/rule_twin_witness.rish
#
# WHY. This tree writes each standing rule twice: `.claude/rules/<name>.md` for Zed and Claude
# Code, `.cursor/rules/<name>.mdc` for Cursor. Both are read as law by whichever editor drives
# the session, so a pair that disagrees means the tree holds two laws under one name and which
# one governs depends on who opened the editor. The initial measurement at `20260824.112806`
# found **40 pairs, 4 agreeing, 36 drifted** (REDS %194); the scan publishes the living count.
#
# `context/document-mirrors.brix` was built for this and cannot hold these pairs. It proves homes
# BYTE-IDENTICAL, and a `.mdc` twin is a transform rather than a copy -- frontmatter on top, local
# links rewritten, and a closing cross-pointer each file aims at the other on purpose. One pair was
# named in that descriptor; registering the other 39 would red every one of them forever. So the
# transform is declared in the scan and checked, rather than assumed.
#
# TWO READINGS, SPLIT `20260908.034712`, BECAUSE ONE NUMBER ANSWERED TWO QUESTIONS. The elder
# gate was the single figure 36. On `20260908` the tree held 51 pairs and 38 drifted, so it
# refused -- while drift among the same 40 pairs it was seated over had FALLEN 36 -> 35. The count
# was carrying both *did a standing pair drift further* and *how many pairs are there*, and the
# second moved every time a hand wrote a new rule. `tools/fixtures/r/rule_twin_cohort.txt` names
# the 40 pairs of the seating day -- a census of a closed day, so it can never grow -- and the
# scan counts inside it and outside it separately.
#
# WHAT IS GATED: `cohort_drifted`, under a ceiling that only ever falls, so the tree can only get
# closer to agreement among the pairs that have had the chance. An empty corpus refuses, since a
# reading over no pairs finds no drift and would report clean while measuring nothing. A missing
# cohort roster refuses too, since a meter that cannot tell a standing pair from a new one would
# read all 51 as arrivals and gate on nothing.
#
# WHAT IS REPORTED AND NEVER GATED: `arrival_drifted`, with each pair named. Reconciling a pair
# means reading both sides and deciding which sentence is the law -- the drift runs in BOTH
# directions. `.cursor/rules/reds-first.mdc` names `work-in-progress/REDS.md`, a path that moved
# to `construction/`, so that twin is behind. `.cursor/rules/send-word.mdc` carries an `ls-remote`
# pre-push guard and a two-remote push discipline that `.claude/rules/send-word.md` has never
# held, so that twin is AHEAD. A bulk merge in either direction would silently delete a live
# safety rule, so that decision is Keaton's word. Gating arrivals would therefore refuse the
# ordinary act of writing a new rule while the only lawful repair waits on a word -- a gate on new
# writing in a ratchet's clothes.
#
# THE SPLIT IS ALSO THE HONEST READING. 8 of the 11 pairs born since the seating arrived AGREEING,
# against 5 of 40 among the elders. The habit changed, and one absolute could not see it.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT REACH. Which side of a drifted pair is RIGHT. That is a reading, and this is a
# count. `sh tools/fixtures/r/rule_twin_scan.sh diff <name>` prints what one pair disagrees about.
#
# THE TRANSFORM WIDENED 20260910.201341, BY THREE STEPS. A link into the rules room names the
# room the READER's own editor loads, so `.cursor/rules/` and `.claude/rules/` read as one
# directory; a GFM table delimiter run canonicalizes, since `|------|` and `|---|` render one way;
# and `&lt;` reads as `<`. The pair-level reading that proposed them was WRONG -- the sibling-room
# spelling stood among the differing lines of 24 of 48 pairs, which reads like half the census
# being noise, and at LINE level it is 12 lines of 1,512, because such a line almost always
# differs for a second reason too. One cohort pair flipped, `vocabulary-survey`, whose whole
# disagreement was a table rule. A difference that is not a difference should never stand in a
# count somebody is asked to act on, however small it turns out to be.
#
# THREE COHORT PAIRS WENT AGREE -> DRIFT BETWEEN 20260908 AND 20260910, which is what carried the
# reading 35 -> 38 and refused the meter. `exec-bit` by one swept word, `gauge-style` by a
# nine-line paragraph the twin never received, `placeholder-ship-names` by a Radiant pass applied
# to one side. Each is a lap editing the page its own editor reads and leaving the other bench
# behind, so bringing the three twins forward completes a half-made edit rather than deciding
# which sentence is the law, and needed no word. The ceiling stands at 34.
#
# Pen: tools/fixtures/r/rule_twin_control.sh -- twenty-four behaviors on real directories in a
# throwaway pen, ten refusals bitten and fourteen honest readings left free, the cohort ceiling
# proven from both sides, the arrival leg proven to report rather than refuse, and each of the
# three new transform steps mutation-proven: removed in turn, each bites exactly its own free leg. Run from the
# repository root.

say "rule-twin: Language EN -- a rule written twice is read as law twice, so both copies must say one thing."
say "rule-twin: Style Gauge, Meter -- every count below is the scan's own output, read from the tree rather than recited here."
say "rule-twin: Lens TAME -- the transform is declared rather than assumed, the corpus size is published beside the verdict, and the reconciliation this cannot make is named as a gate rather than attempted."

let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/rule_twin_scan.sh" "census"]
say scan.out
assert scan.ok else "rule-twin: cohort drift stands above a ceiling that only ever falls -- read the scan's own lines"
assert scan.out contains "verdict=ok" else "rule-twin: the census must agree with itself"
assert (scan.out contains "rule_pairs=0" == false) else "rule-twin: a zero-pair reading would report clean over an empty roster"
assert (scan.out contains "verdict=empty_corpus" == false) else "rule-twin: a meter over nothing must say so rather than pass"
assert (scan.out contains "verdict=cohort_absent" == false) else "rule-twin: a meter that cannot name its cohort reads every pair as an arrival and gates on nothing"
assert (scan.out contains "cohort_pairs=0" == false) else "rule-twin: an empty cohort would leave the gated leg measuring nothing"
assert scan.out contains "arrival_drifted=" else "rule-twin: growth must be reported beside the gated reading rather than folded into it"

let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/r/rule_twin_control.sh"]
say control.out
assert control.ok else "rule-twin: the control must complete"
assert control.out contains "control_fail=0" else "rule-twin: every planted behavior must read as written"
assert control.out contains "control_verdict=ok" else "rule-twin: the control must agree with itself"
assert control.out contains "1 free: a pair identical but for Cursor frontmatter reads as agreeing -- ok" else "rule-twin: frontmatter is the twin's own shape and must pass free"
assert control.out contains "2 free: a link written .mdc in the twin reads as agreeing -- ok" else "rule-twin: a rewritten link target must pass free"
assert control.out contains "4 free: the closing cross-pointer is dropped from both rather than demanded equal -- ok" else "rule-twin: each file names the other there, so agreeing would make them wrong"
assert control.out contains "5 free: a blank-line reflow reads as agreeing -- ok" else "rule-twin: formatting must never be called drift"
assert control.out contains "6 bitten: one changed word in the body counts as drift -- ok" else "rule-twin: a transform too generous to see one word is worse than no meter"
assert control.out contains "7 bitten: a whole paragraph present on one side only counts as drift -- ok" else "rule-twin: the send-word shape, a rule on one side only, must bite"
assert control.out contains "8 bitten: a stale path on the twin counts as drift -- ok" else "rule-twin: the reds-first shape, a path that moved, must bite"
assert control.out contains "9 bitten: cohort drift above the ceiling refuses -- ok" else "rule-twin: the ceiling must bite from above"
assert control.out contains "10 free: cohort drift at exactly the ceiling passes free -- ok" else "rule-twin: the ceiling must pass free from below, or it is not a ceiling"
assert control.out contains "11 bitten: an empty corpus reads as empty rather than clean -- ok" else "rule-twin: a meter over nothing must say so"
assert control.out contains "12 free: a Cursor rule with no Claude canonical is counted rather than ignored -- ok" else "rule-twin: an orphan twin must be counted, since it is law with no canonical"
assert control.out contains "13 free: a drifted arrival raises the total and leaves the cohort reading alone -- ok" else "rule-twin: growth entering the gated reading is the whole fault this split repairs"
assert control.out contains "14 free: and the tree passes free at a ceiling the elder absolute would have refused -- ok" else "rule-twin: the repair must be shown to change the verdict, not only the wording"
assert control.out contains "15 free: a pair born outside the cohort, drifted, is reported rather than refused -- ok" else "rule-twin: gating arrivals would refuse the ordinary act of writing a new rule"
assert control.out contains "15b free: and it is named in the reading rather than passed over -- ok" else "rule-twin: an ungated reading that names nobody is an unread reading"
assert control.out contains "16 bitten: a cohort name that is no longer a pair reads as absent -- ok" else "rule-twin: a rule retired and a pair reconciled are two facts wearing one appearance"
assert control.out contains "18 free: a link into the rules room naming each editor's own copy reads as agreeing -- ok" else "rule-twin: one instruction spelled for two benches is the transform rather than a disagreement"
assert control.out contains "19 bitten: and a changed word on that same line still counts as drift -- ok" else "rule-twin: a step that reads two spellings as one must still see a real change riding beside it"
assert control.out contains "20 free: a table delimiter row written to a different width reads as agreeing -- ok" else "rule-twin: a delimiter run of any length past three renders one way, so it says one thing"
assert control.out contains "21 bitten: and a changed cell in the table below it still counts as drift -- ok" else "rule-twin: canonicalizing the rule must never quiet the row beneath it"
assert control.out contains "22 free: an escaped &lt; reads as the character it names -- ok" else "rule-twin: an escaped character and the character are one character"
assert control.out contains "23 bitten: and a changed bound beside it still counts as drift -- ok" else "rule-twin: an entity read as its character must still leave the number beside it visible"
assert control.out contains "17 bitten: an absent cohort roster refuses rather than reading every pair as an arrival -- ok" else "rule-twin: a meter that cannot name its cohort must refuse rather than gate on nothing"

say ""
say "GREEN: rule-twin -- the living rule pairs were measured under a declared transform, drift among the seated cohort held under a ceiling that only falls, growth reported beside it rather than folded into it, proven both ways across twenty-four behaviors, the three transform steps seated 20260910 each planted twice so a spelling read as one still bites a real change riding on its line, and the reconciliation itself surfaced as a gate rather than attempted."
